WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.195
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.195 --> 00:00:01.140
DALE MARKOWITZ: The
neat thing about working

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.510
in machine learning is that
every few years, somebody

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.030
invents something crazy that
makes you totally reconsider

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:09.365
what's possible, like
models that can play

00:00:09.365 --> 00:00:12.090
Go or generate
hyper-realistic faces.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.740
And today, the
mind-blowing discovery

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.157
that's rocking
everyone's world is

00:00:15.157 --> 00:00:17.760
a type of neural network
called a transformer.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.310
Transformers are models that
can translate text, write

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.220
poems and op-eds, and even
generate computer code.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.680
They could be used in biology
to solve the protein folding

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:26.820
problem.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.650
Transformers are like
this magical machine

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.680
learning hammer that seems to
make every problem into a nail.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.540
If you've heard of the
trendy new ML models

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.080
BERT, or GPT-3, or T5,
all of these models

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.060
are based on transformers.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.373
So if you want to stay
hip in machine learning

00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:43.290
and especially in natural
language processing,

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:44.935
you have to know
about the transformer.

00:00:44.935 --> 00:00:46.560
So in this video,
I'm going to tell you

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.780
about what transformers
are, how they work,

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.730
and why they've
been so impactful.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:51.910
Let's get to it.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:53.310
So what is a transformer?

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.710
It's a type of neural
network architecture.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.170
To recap, neural networks
are a very effective type

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.210
of model for analyzing
complicated data

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:02.712
types, like images,
videos, audio, and text.

00:01:02.712 --> 00:01:05.129
But there are different types
of neural networks optimized

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:06.330
for different types of data.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.497
Like if you're analyzing
images, you would typically

00:01:08.497 --> 00:01:10.810
use a convolutional
neural network,

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:12.300
which is designed
to vaguely mimic

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.700
the way that the human
brain processes vision.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.710
And since around
2012, neural networks

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.840
have been really good
at solving vision tasks,

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.250
like identifying
objects in photos.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:23.610
But for a long time, we didn't
have anything comparably

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.310
good for analyzing language,
whether for translation,

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.843
or text summarization,
or text generation.

00:01:28.843 --> 00:01:31.260
And this is a problem, because
language is the primary way

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.310
that humans communicate.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.470
You see, until transformers
came around, the way

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.390
we used deep learning
to understand text

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.360
was with a type of model called
a Recurrent Neural Network,

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.927
or an RNN, that looked
something like this.

00:01:42.927 --> 00:01:44.760
Let's say you wanted
to translate a sentence

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.260
from English to French.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.630
An RNN would take as
input an English sentence

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.580
and process the
words one at a time,

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.820
and then sequentially spit
out their French counterparts.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.770
The keyword here is sequential.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.960
In language, the order
of words matters,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.220
and you can't just
shuffle them around.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.157
For example, the sentence
Jane went looking for trouble

00:02:03.157 --> 00:02:05.490
means something very different
than the sentence Trouble

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.132
went looking for Jane.

00:02:07.132 --> 00:02:09.090
So any model that's going
to deal with language

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.910
has to capture word order,
and recurrent neural networks

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.550
do this by looking at one
word at a time sequentially.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.570
But RNNs had a lot of problems.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:17.970
First, they never
really did well

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.090
at handling large sequences
of text, like long paragraphs

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:21.798
or essays.

00:02:21.798 --> 00:02:24.090
By the time they were analyzing
the end of a paragraph,

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.070
they'd forget what
happened in the beginning.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:29.070
And even worse, RNNs were
pretty hard to train.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.870
Because they process
words sequentially,

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.610
they couldn't
paralellize well, which

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:34.860
means that you couldn't just
speed them up by throwing

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:35.902
lots of GPUs at them.

00:02:35.902 --> 00:02:37.860
And when you have a model
that's slow to train,

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.090
you can't train it on
all that much data.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.330
This is where the transformer
changed everything.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:45.210
They're a model developed in
2017 by researchers at Google

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.460
and the University of Toronto,
and they were initially

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.020
designed to do translation.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.730
But unlike recurrent
neural networks,

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.097
you could really efficiently
paralellize transformers.

00:02:53.097 --> 00:02:54.930
And that meant that
with the right hardware,

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.970
you could train some
really big models.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.080
How big?

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.250
Really big.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:02.100
Remember GPT-3, that model
that writes poetry and code,

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.750
and has conversations?

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.760
That was trained on almost
45 terabytes of text data,

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.655
including almost the
entire public web.

00:03:09.655 --> 00:03:12.600
[WHISTLES] So if you remember
anything about transformers,

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:13.500
let it be this.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.290
Combine a model that scales
really well with a huge data

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.790
set and the results will
probably blow your mind.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:20.850
So how do these
things actually work?

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:24.270
From the diagram in the paper,
it should be pretty clear.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.560
Or maybe not.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.600
Actually, it's simpler
than you might think.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.142
There are three main
innovations that

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:30.780
make this model work so well.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.360
Positional encodings and
attention, and specifically,

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.098
a type of attention
called self-attention.

00:03:36.098 --> 00:03:37.890
Let's start by talking
about the first one,

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.343
positional encodings.

00:03:39.343 --> 00:03:41.010
Let's say we're trying
to translate text

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.150
from English to French.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.160
Positional encodings is
the idea that instead

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.810
of looking at
words sequentially,

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.310
you take each word
in your sentence,

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.268
and before you feed it
into the neural network,

00:03:49.268 --> 00:03:50.610
you slap a number on it--

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:52.920
1, 2, 3, depending
on what number

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.348
the word is in the sentence.

00:03:54.348 --> 00:03:55.890
In other words, you
store information

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:57.660
about word order
in the data itself,

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.910
rather than in the
structure of the network.

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.250
Then as you train the
network on lots of text data,

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:05.862
it learns how to interpret
those positional encodings.

00:04:05.862 --> 00:04:08.070
In this way, the neural
network learns the importance

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:10.747
of word order from the data.

00:04:10.747 --> 00:04:12.330
This is a high level
way to understand

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.340
positional encodings,
but it's an innovation

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.290
that really helped make
transformers easier

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:18.360
to train than RNNs.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:19.980
The next innovation
in this paper

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.623
is a concept called
attention, which

00:04:21.623 --> 00:04:24.040
you'll see used everywhere in
machine learning these days.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.550
In fact, the title of the
original transformer paper

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.600
is "Attention Is All You Need."

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:31.830
So the agreement on the
European economic area

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.290
was signed in August 1992.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:35.610
Did you know that?

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.980
That's the example sentence
given in the original paper.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.840
And remember, the
original transformer

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.790
was designed for translation.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.580
Now imagine trying to translate
that sentence to French.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.580
One bad way to translate text
is to try to translate each word

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:48.720
one for one.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.400
But in French, some
words are flipped,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.300
like in the French translation,
European comes before economic.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.220
Plus, French is a
language that has gendered

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.700
agreement between words.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.720
So the word [FRENCH] needs
to be in the feminine form

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.580
to match with [FRENCH].

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.040
The attention mechanism is
a neural network structure

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.770
that allows a text model to
look at every single word

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.270
in the original
sentence when making

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.520
a decision about how to
translate a word in the output

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:12.550
sentence.

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:14.190
In fact, here's a
nice visualization

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.980
from that paper that shows what
words in the input sentence

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.810
the model is
attending to when it

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:22.030
makes predictions about a
word for the output sentence.

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:25.120
So when the model outputs
the word [FRENCH],,

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.885
it's looking at the input
words European and economic.

00:05:28.885 --> 00:05:30.510
You can think of this
diagram as a sort

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.215
of heat map for attention.

00:05:32.215 --> 00:05:33.840
And how does the
model know which words

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:35.790
it should be attending to?

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.457
It's something that's
learned over time from data.

00:05:38.457 --> 00:05:41.040
By seeing thousands of examples
of French and English sentence

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.960
pairs, the model
learns about gender,

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.730
and word order, and
plurality, and all

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:46.140
of that grammatical stuff.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.600
So we talked about two key
transformer innovations,

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.180
positional encoding
and attention.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:54.180
But actually, attention had
been invented before this paper.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.280
The real innovation in
transformers was something

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:00.320
called self-attention, a twist
on traditional attention.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:02.070
The type of attention
we just talked about

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.600
had to do with aligning
words in English and French,

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:06.477
which is really important
for translation.

00:06:06.477 --> 00:06:08.310
But what if you're just
trying to understand

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.980
the underlying meaning
in language so that you

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:14.683
can build a network that can do
any number of language tasks?

00:06:14.683 --> 00:06:16.350
What's incredible
about neural networks,

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.830
like transformers, is that as
they analyze tons of text data,

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:22.650
they begin to build up this
internal representation

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:25.410
or understanding of
language automatically.

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:28.500
They might learn, for example,
that the words programmer,

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.660
and software engineer,
and software developer

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.010
are all synonymous.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:34.470
And they might also naturally
learn the rules of grammar,

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:36.690
and gender, and
tense, and so on.

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:38.880
The better this internal
representation of language

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.547
the neural network
learns, the better it

00:06:40.547 --> 00:06:42.690
will be at any language task.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:45.150
And it turns out that attention
can be a very effective way

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.370
to get a neural network
to understand language

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:50.710
if it's turned on the
input text itself.

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:51.900
Let me give you an example.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:53.770
Take these two sentences--

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:55.320
Server, can I have the check?

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:58.320
Versus, Looks like I
just crashed the server.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.900
The word server here means
two very different things.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:02.400
And I know that,
because I'm looking

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:05.100
at the context of the
surrounding words.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:06.780
Self-attention allows
a neural network

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:10.210
to understand a word in the
context of the words around it.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:12.030
So when a model
processes the word server

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:13.470
in the first
sentence, it might be

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:15.810
attending to the
word check, which

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:19.655
helps it disambiguate from a
human server versus a mail one.

00:07:19.655 --> 00:07:21.030
In the second
sentence, the model

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:23.155
might be attending to the
word crashed to determine

00:07:23.155 --> 00:07:24.627
that the server is a machine.

00:07:24.627 --> 00:07:26.460
Self-attention can also
help neural networks

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:29.070
disambiguate words,
recognize parts of speech,

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:31.350
and even identify word tense.

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:34.560
This, in a nutshell, is the
value of self-attention.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.750
So to summarize,
transformers boil down

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:41.160
to positional encodings,
attention, and self-attention.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.190
Of course, this is a 10,000-foot
look at transformers.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:45.960
But how are they
actually useful?

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:48.150
One of the most popular
transformer-based models

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:50.490
is called BERT, which was
invented just around the time

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:53.010
that I joined Google in 2018.

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:55.090
BERT was trained on
a massive text corpus

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:57.630
and has become this sort
of general pocketknife

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:01.230
for NLP that can be adapted
to a bunch of different tasks,

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:03.480
like text summarization,
question answering,

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:06.750
classification, and
finding similar sentences.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:09.510
It's used in Google Search to
help understand search queries,

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:12.270
and it powers a lot of
Google Cloud's NLP tools,

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:15.160
like Google Cloud
AutoML Natural Language.

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:17.580
BERT also proved that you
could build very good models

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.710
on unlabeled data,
like text scraped

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:21.510
from Wikipedia or Reddit.

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:23.310
This is called
semi-supervised learning,

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:27.340
and it's a big trend in
machine learning right now.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:29.700
So if I've sold you about
how cool transformers are,

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:31.800
you might want to start
using them in your app.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:32.909
No problem.

00:08:32.909 --> 00:08:35.700
TensorFlow Hub is a great place
to grab pretrained transformer

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:36.960
models, like BERT.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.270
You can download them for
free in multiple language

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:41.710
and drop them straight
into your app.

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:44.370
You can also check out the
popular transformers Python

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:46.328
library, built by the
company Hugging Face.

00:08:46.328 --> 00:08:48.120
That's one of the
community's favorite ways

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:49.920
to train and use
transformer models.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:51.570
For more transformer
tips, check out

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:54.750
my blog post linked below,
and thanks for watching.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:58.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

